        <div class="stat-box">
          <div class="stat-number">1.6g/kg</div>
          <div class="stat-label">protein target during a calorie deficit — higher than maintenance</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-box">
          <div class="stat-number">20–30%</div>
          <div class="stat-label">of protein calories burned just in digestion — highest of any macro</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-box">
          <div class="stat-number">2–3x</div>
          <div class="stat-label">resistance training per week — the signal that tells your body to keep muscle</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>The Trap Most Men Over 40 Fall Into</h2>

      <p>
        Here's the pattern. A man over 40 decides to lose 20 pounds. He cuts calories — eats less, skips snacks, maybe cuts carbs. He loses weight. Then something goes wrong that he can't quite explain.
      </p>

      <p>
        He feels weaker. He looks smaller but not leaner. The weight creeps back. Six months later he weighs roughly what he did before, but his body composition is actually worse — less muscle, more fat — than when he started.
      </p>

      <div class="fix-grid">
        <div class="fix-card problem">
          <div class="fc-label">✗ What happened</div>
          <div class="fc-text">He lost weight — but a meaningful share of it was muscle, not fat. In a calorie deficit with low protein and no lifting, his body broke down muscle along with fat.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fix-card solution">
          <div class="fc-label">✓ What prevents it</div>
          <div class="fc-text">High protein plus resistance training during a deficit helps the body hold onto muscle, so more of what you lose comes from fat. The deficit stays the same; what you lose changes.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fix-card problem">
          <div class="fc-label">✗ The downstream effect</div>
          <div class="fc-text">He's weaker, and when the weight comes back (driven mostly by appetite), it returns mostly as fat. Repeat the cycle a few times and each round leaves him with less muscle.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fix-card solution">
          <div class="fc-label">✓ The better outcome</div>
          <div class="fc-text">He keeps his strength, and the body he ends up with after the cut is leaner and stronger, not just lighter. That makes the new weight easier to live with and keep.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        This isn't a discipline failure. It's a protein and training failure. And it matters more after 40 because of <a href="https://snapprotein.com/blog/anabolic-resistance-after-40/">anabolic resistance</a> — the biological mechanism that makes aging muscle less efficient at using dietary protein. You need more protein to get the same protective effect, not less.
      </p>

      <div class="link-box">
        <strong>The biology behind this</strong>
        <a href="https://snapprotein.com/blog/sarcopenia-men-over-40/">Sarcopenia — why men over 40 lose muscle faster than they realize, and what stops it →</a>
      </div>

      <h2>Why Your Protein Target Goes Up When You're Cutting</h2>

      <p>
        During normal eating — maintenance calories — the research-backed protein target for men over 40 is around 1.2g per kilogram of bodyweight per day. That's what it takes to maintain muscle against the effects of <a href="https://snapprotein.com/blog/anabolic-resistance-after-40/">anabolic resistance</a> and age-related muscle loss.
      </p>

      <p>
        During a calorie deficit, that target rises to <strong>1.6g/kg</strong>. The reason is straightforward: when calories are restricted, the body breaks down more protein, including muscle. Eating more protein replaces it, and lifting gives the body a reason to keep the muscle. A 2016 meta-analysis of 20 trials in adults over 50 found that those on higher-protein diets kept more lean mass and lost more fat while losing weight. If you're already lean or cutting aggressively, you can go as high as about 2.2g/kg (1g per pound).
      </p>

      <!-- TARGET CARDS -->
      <div class="target-grid">
        <div class="target-card">
          <div class="tc-label">Maintenance target</div>
          <div class="tc-number">1.2g/kg</div>
          <div class="tc-desc">Preserves muscle at normal calorie intake. The baseline for men over 40 not in a deficit.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="target-card">
          <div class="tc-label">Weight loss target</div>
          <div class="tc-number">1.6g/kg</div>
          <div class="tc-desc">Protects muscle during a calorie deficit. Higher requirement, not lower. Most men get this backwards.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Most men do the opposite. They cut calories and protein simultaneously — smaller portions across the board. That's the formula for losing muscle. The right formula is to cut calories while holding or increasing protein, and lifting weights, which shifts more of the loss toward fat stores instead of muscle tissue.
      </p>

      <div class="pull-quote">
        <p>"Cut calories. Hold protein. That's the whole strategy. Everything else is secondary."</p>
      </div>

      <h2>What This Looks Like in Real Numbers</h2>

      <p>
        Here's how the 1.6g/kg target translates across the weight range where most men over 40 land. Compare it to the maintenance target and what the average man actually eats (about 93g a day for men 40–59 and 82g for men 60+, per U.S. government survey data).
      </p>

