      </div>

      <h2>Three Reasons High Protein Accelerates Fat Loss</h2>

      <p>
        Muscle preservation is the primary reason — but it's not the only one. High protein intake during a deficit works on several levels at once.
      </p>

      <!-- COMPARE TABLE -->
      <div class="compare-table-wrap">
        <table class="compare-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Mechanism</th>
              <th>What it means</th>
              <th>Impact on fat loss</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Muscle preservation</strong></td>
              <td>With lifting, protein helps the body keep muscle in a deficit</td>
              <td class="good">More of the weight lost is fat; strength stays</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Thermic effect</strong></td>
              <td>About 20–30% of protein calories are burned in digestion — highest of any macro</td>
              <td class="good">Burns more calories from the same food</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Satiety</strong></td>
              <td>Protein lowers the hunger hormone ghrelin and raises fullness hormones like GLP-1 and PYY</td>
              <td class="good">Easier to maintain the deficit</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Low protein approach</strong></td>
              <td>Body loses more muscle alongside fat; hunger is harder to manage</td>
              <td class="bad">Weaker, and regained weight comes back as fat</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The thermic effect is worth understanding. Your body uses about 5–10% of carbohydrate calories and 0–3% of fat calories to digest them. For protein, it's about 20–30%. That means 500 calories of protein nets out closer to 350–400 calories. It's a built-in calorie discount on the one macro that also protects your muscle.</p>

      <p>
        A note on metabolism: losing muscle does slow your resting metabolism, but only a little. A pound of muscle burns about 6 calories a day at rest, so losing 5 pounds of muscle costs about 30 calories a day. The bigger reasons to protect muscle are strength, how you look and function, and not ending the diet "skinny-fat." Regaining weight after a diet is driven mostly by appetite and the body adapting to eating less, which is another reason protein's effect on hunger matters.
      </p>

      <h2>The Right Way to Structure a Deficit After 40</h2>

      <p>
        This isn't complicated, but the sequencing matters. Most men approach it backwards.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Set your protein target first — 1.6g/kg of bodyweight per day. This is non-negotiable during a cut.<br><br>
        <strong>Step 2:</strong> Create a moderate calorie deficit (300–500 calories below maintenance), aiming to lose about 0.5–1% of your bodyweight per week. Faster cuts take more muscle with them.<br><br>
        <strong>Step 3:</strong> Let the deficit come from reducing fat and carbohydrates, not protein. Protein stays high regardless of what else changes.<br><br>
        <strong>Step 4:</strong> Lift 2–3 times a week. Muscle under load is muscle that stays. Dieting without resistance training loses noticeably more muscle, even with good protein. Training is the signal; protein is the raw material.</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        The men who lose fat and keep muscle after 40 aren't doing anything exotic. They're running a moderate deficit with high protein and some form of resistance training. That's the entire framework. The problem is most weight loss advice hands them a calorie number and sends them on their way — with no guidance on where that deficit should come from.
      </p>

      <h2>What This Means for Tracking</h2>

      <p>
        If you're in a deficit, the stakes for hitting your protein target are higher than at maintenance. Missing by 30g when you're trying to lose weight isn't just leaving muscle-building on the table — it's actively risking the muscle you already have.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is exactly why visibility matters. <a href="https://snapprotein.com/blog/hard-to-hit-protein-goal-after-40/">Hitting 120g+ every day is already hard after 40</a> — hitting 130–150g while eating fewer total calories is harder. You need to know where you stand at lunch, not at dinner when it's too late to course-correct.
      </p>

      <p>
        One number. Tracked simply. Checked mid-day. That's the feedback loop that makes the difference between a cut that works and one that costs you muscle you'll spend years trying to rebuild.
      </p>

