        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>How much protein should men over 40 eat to lose weight?</h3>
          <p>Aim for about 1.6g of protein per kilogram of bodyweight per day while losing weight, up from the 1.2g/kg baseline. Leaner men or more aggressive cuts can go up to about 2.2g/kg (1g per pound). Combined with resistance training, this helps you keep muscle so more of the weight you lose comes from fat.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Why do men over 40 lose muscle when they diet?</h3>
          <p>In a calorie deficit, the body breaks down some muscle along with fat, especially when protein is low and there's no resistance training to give muscle a reason to stay. After 40, anabolic resistance makes muscle less responsive to protein, so the loss can be larger. Fast, aggressive diets make it worse.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Does eating more protein help with weight loss after 40?</h3>
          <p>Yes, in three ways. A 2016 meta-analysis of adults over 50 found that those on higher-protein diets kept more lean mass and lost more fat while losing weight. Protein also has the highest thermic effect of any macronutrient (about 20–30% of its calories are used in digestion), and it's the most filling, which makes a deficit easier to stick to.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Can you lose fat and keep muscle after 40?</h3>
          <p>Yes. The combination that works is about 1.6g/kg of protein per day, a moderate calorie deficit (losing roughly 0.5–1% of bodyweight per week), and resistance training 2–3 times a week. Men who are newer to lifting can sometimes even gain muscle while losing fat.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>What happens to metabolism when men over 40 diet without enough protein?</h3>
          <p>Some of the weight lost is muscle, and metabolism does drop a little, but less than most people think: a pound of muscle burns only about 6 calories a day at rest. The bigger costs are lost strength and function, and ending the diet with less muscle and proportionally more fat. When weight comes back, it's mostly driven by appetite and the body adapting to the diet, and it returns mostly as fat.</p>
        </div>

